<DocsLayout title="Advanced DSPy guide | dotfetch" description="Approving gated calls, per-run selection, run context and error modes for the dotfetch DSPy integration.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Advanced framework guide</p>
  <h1>Advanced DSPy</h1>
  <p class="lead">Approving confirmation-gated calls, selecting tools per call, and run context.</p>
  <p><a class="pill" href="/dotfetch/docs/frameworks/dspy/">← Basic DSPy usage</a></p>

  <h2>Approving gated calls</h2>
  <p>DSPy has no human-in-the-loop mechanism of its own, so the adapter takes an approver. It is asked about confirmation-gated tools only (<code>requires_confirmation=True</code> or a <code>SecurityPolicy(require_confirmation=[...])</code> pattern).</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from dotfetch.dspy import ApprovalRequest, DSPyAdapter

def approve(request: ApprovalRequest) -> bool:
    # request.tool_name, request.arguments, request.description, request.module
    return input(f"Run {request.tool_name}({request.arguments})? [y/N] ").strip() == "y"

tools = await DSPyAdapter(fetcher, approve=approve).select("refund an order")`} />
  <p><code>True</code> runs the call once, with only dotfetch's confirmation gate cleared; every other policy check still applies. Anything else, or an approver that raises, blocks the call and tells the model it was declined. With no approver, a gated call reports that it needs approval. <code>error_mode="raise"</code> never asks the approver.</p>

  <h2>Per-run selection</h2>
  <p><code>dspy.ReAct</code> fixes its tools, and writes their names into its instructions, when it is constructed, and DSPy has no hook before each model call. So selection is per call: <code>agent_builder()</code> builds a fresh <code>ReAct</code> with tools picked for each query, using the adapter's <code>error_formatter</code> and <code>approve</code>. If retrieval fails, the pinned tools are still offered (with a <code>WARNING</code>).</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`builder = DSPyAdapter(fetcher, approve=approve).agent_builder(
    "question -> answer", max_iters=5, always_include=["get_order_status"]
)
react = await builder.build("What's the weather in Tokyo?")
pred = react(question="What's the weather in Tokyo?")`} />

  <h2>Run context</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from dotfetch.dspy import current_dspy_module

@fetcher.register
def audit_note(text: str) -> str:
    """Record a note against the running task."""
    module = current_dspy_module()     # the dspy.ReAct running this call, or None
    return log.write(type(module).__name__, text)`} />
  <p>The same object is <code>current_tool_invocation().native_context</code>. DSPy has no run or call ids to pass on; the active OpenTelemetry span becomes the <code>ToolCall</code>'s trace.</p>
</DocsLayout>
